SUMMARY:Cities of Islamic Art: From Historical Visual Systems to the Format
 ion of Contemporary Islamic Art in North America
LOCATION:MIT\, 33 MASSACHUSETTS AVE\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:This lecture uses Cities of Islamic Art\, Vol. 2 as both an obj
 ect and a methodology to examine Islamic art as a mobile system of visual 
 knowledge rather than a fixed historical style. Beginning with historical 
 surfaces from Isfahan\, Istanbul\, Damascus\, Cairo\, and Granada\, the ta
 lk traces how motifs migrate across ceramics\, tiles\, manuscripts\, and a
 rchitecture to form the fabric of visual culture and heritage known as “
 Islamic Art.”\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/cities-of-islamic-art\nFro
 m Pushpin Boston: https://pushpin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/cities-of-islamic-art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3016118f7acdb644068392d90a2a594cc46c6f0c@pushpin.city
DTSTAMP:20261011T010050Z
DTSTART:20261028T210000Z
DTEND:20261028T230000Z
SUMMARY:MIT Music Tech Speaker Series: Andrew McPherson
LOCATION:MIT\, 182 MEMORIAL DR (REAR)\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:Enacted\, not Extracted: Designing and Performing with Music-An
 alytical Concepts This is a talk about designing musical instruments and t
 he delicate interplay of artistic and technical ways of thinking. All inst
 ruments\, and especially digital ones\, entail a balance between design an
 d analysis\, with instruments relying on symbolic or numerical models of m
 usic in order to create it\, as exemplified in the venerable MIDI standard
 . However\, no system is ever devoid of sociocultural context\, and even t
 he simplest technologies when examined in situ can exhibit fascinating com
 plexity.\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/mit-music-tech-speaker-series-and
 rew-mcpherson\nFrom Pushpin Boston: https://pushpin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/mit-music-tech-speaker-series-andrew-mcp
 herson
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ce87ba3a9b9c1fe17e1a49b5edc098692aee2da5@pushpin.city
DTSTAMP:20261011T010050Z
DTSTART:20261028T211500Z
DTEND:20261028T231500Z
SUMMARY:The Ambivalences of Return: Diasporic Utopias and Anti-Migrant Poli
 cies
LOCATION:MIT\, 160 MEMORIAL DR\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:"Return" is a central notion for thinking about the diasporic c
 ondition in general (W. Safran). In the "radical imaginations" (R.D.G. Kel
 ley) of the Black Americas of the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries\
 , from Black nationalism to Pan-Africanism\, as well as in African anticol
 onialisms\, "return" refers to a shared political dream: making Africa a l
 and of hospitality for all Africans and/or Black people dispersed across t
 he world. Return carries an ideal of reparative justice: reuniting\, on th
 e land from which they were torn\, those whom slavery and colonization had
  separated. Within these traditions\, however\, the idea is fraught with t
 ensions\, and for those who experience return to the continent\, it often 
 appears as a myth (S. Hartman\, M. Condé). In the aftermath of the Second
  World War\, the idea of "return" was elevated to the status of a right. T
 he Universal Declaration of Human Rights of 1948 recognizes everyone's rig
 ht to return to their country\, and that same year\, in the context of the
  war in Palestine\, United Nations General Assembly Resolution 194 affirme
 d it for Palestinian refugees. From then on\, the term became the object o
 f competing claims to the same land\, as illustrated two years later by th
 e Israeli Law of Return.\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/the-ambivalences-
 of-return-diasporic-utopias-and-anti-migrant-policies\nFrom Pushpin Boston
 : https://pushpin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/the-ambivalences-of-return-diasporic-uto
 pias-and-anti-migrant-policies
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f47c8cdf7d68f297604e7c1a5a7d17871360f210@pushpin.city
DTSTAMP:20261011T010050Z
DTSTART:20261029T220000Z
DTEND:20261030T000000Z
SUMMARY:Anooradha Iyer Siddiqi and Rachel Lee
LOCATION:MIT\, 134 MASSACHUSETTS Avenue\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:Anooradha Iyer Siddiqi and Rachel Lee Presented with the HTC Fo
 rum Part of the MIT Fall 2026 Architecture Lecture Series The Work of Coll
 aboration Collaboration has often occupied an ambiguous place within archi
 tectural history\, a discipline that continues to privilege individual aut
 horship\, singular expertise and archival authority. Collaboration can uns
 ettle these privileges. By bringing researchers together it reshapes the q
 uestions we ask\, the evidence we value and the histories we are able to w
 rite.\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/anooradha-iyer-siddiq-and-rachel-lee
 \nFrom Pushpin Boston: https://pushpin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/anooradha-iyer-siddiq-and-rachel-lee
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddd4b1f1fd8bba3d9bdfff28cf3c4c5f0d38b689@pushpin.city
DTSTAMP:20261011T010050Z
DTSTART:20261029T223000Z
DTEND:20261030T003000Z
SUMMARY:MIT / French Library Author Talks: Camille de Peretti on  Portrait 
 of an Unknown Woman
LOCATION:MIT\, 53 Marlborough St\, Boston\, MA 02116
DESCRIPTION:In 1910\, Gustav Klimt painted a young woman\, then painted ove
 r her portrait. The painting was stolen in 1997 and unexpectedly resurface
 d two decades later. Camille de Peretti uses this intriguing chapter in ar
 t history to imagine the woman behind the painting and the generations tha
 t follow her.\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/mit-french-library-author-ta
 lks-camille-de-peretti-on-portrait-of-an-unknown-woman\nFrom Pushpin Bosto
 n: https://pushpin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/mit-french-library-author-talks-camille-
 de-peretti-on-portrait-of-an-unknown-woman
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:75680fdedbbd4207e84092d0a6d0b57fe6923f2f@pushpin.city
DTSTAMP:20261011T010050Z
DTSTART:20261105T230000Z
DTEND:20261106T010000Z
SUMMARY:e-flux Architecture—Nick Axel and Nikolaus Hirsch
LOCATION:MIT\, 134 MASSACHUSETTS Avenue\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:e-flux Architecture—Nick Axel and Nikolaus Hirsch Part of the
  MIT Fall 2026 Architecture Lecture Series Presented with the Architecture
  and Urbanism Group Nikolaus Hirsch is co-founder and co-editor of e-flux 
 Architecture\, as well as the director of the museum Kanal Architecture (f
 ormerly CIVA) in Brussels. He was the Dean of Städelschule and Director o
 f the Portikus in Frankfurt and taught at the Architectural Association in
  London. His architectural work includes the award-winning Dresden Synagog
 ue\, Hinzert Document Center\, Bockenheimer Depot Theater (with William Fo
 rsythe)\, Cybermohalla Hub in New-Delhi\, “Do We Dream Under The Same Sk
 y“(with Rirkrit Tiravanija at Art Basel / LUMA Arles) and “Becoming Mo
 nument” in Fukushima. Hirsch has curated numerous exhibitions such “Wo
 hnungsfrage / Housing Question” at the HKW in Berlin and the German Pavi
 lion at the Architecture Biennale in Venice (“2038”). He is the editor
  of the Critical Spatial Practice series at Sternberg Press.\nhttps://cale
 ndar.mit.edu/event/e-flux-architecturenick-axel-and-nikolaus-hirsch\nFrom 
